#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# ///
"""BOSH CPI lifecycle harness.

Exercises the bosh-pve-cpi binary end-to-end against a live PVE cluster by
feeding JSON-RPC requests to ./bin/cpi on stdin (one process per call) and
threading IDs between steps.

Sequence (mirrors the BOSH-CPI Director call order):
    1.  info
    2.  create_stemcell
    2a. create_network          (only when NETWORK_TEST_MODE != off)
    3.  create_vm               (attached to the 2a network when enabled)
    4.  has_vm                  (post-create assertion)
    5.  set_vm_metadata
    6.  create_disk
    6a. has_disk                (post-create assertion, expect true)
    7.  attach_disk
    7a. set_disk_metadata
    7b. resize_disk             (doubles disk size; disk must be attached)
    7c. update_disk             (sets cache=writeback, iothread=true)
    8.  get_disks               (post-attach assertion)
    9.  snapshot_disk
   10.  delete_snapshot          (clean mode; detach_disk in bypass mode)
   11.  detach_disk              (clean mode; delete_snapshot in bypass mode)
   11c-11l. parked-disk pass    (only when PARKED_DISK_TEST != off)
   12.  delete_disk
   12a. has_disk                (post-delete assertion, expect false)
   13.  reboot_vm               (soft graceful reboot, default config)
   14.  reboot_vm               (hard reset, reboot_mode=hard)
   15.  delete_vm
   16.  delete_stemcell
   16a. delete_network          (only when NETWORK_TEST_MODE != off)

Independent PVE-state verification: beyond the CPI's JSON-RPC return values,
selected steps assert real cluster state out-of-band via the PVE REST API
(scripts/_pve_verify.py, same host+auth as CPI_CONFIG): create_network ->
vnet/subnet or bridge present; create_vm -> VM present; create_disk -> volume
present; delete_vm -> VM gone; delete_network -> vnet/bridge gone. A failed
verification aborts the run exactly like a CPI error. The parked-disk pass
leans on this hardest: parking is invisible in the CPI's return values (every
call returns null), so the only proof it happened is the cluster state.

A failure in any step aborts the run and prints the failing JSON-RPC error.
Cleanup is best-effort: resources created before the failure are torn down in
reverse order.

Required environment:
    CPI_CONFIG       path to CPI JSON config (PVE credentials, node, storage)
    STEMCELL_PATH    path to BOSH stemcell tarball (.tgz)

Optional environment:
    CPI_BIN          path to compiled cpi binary  (default: ./bin/cpi)
    AGENT_ID         agent UUID used in create_vm (default: lifecycle-<pid>)
    NETWORK_RANGE    CIDR for the test network    (default: 192.168.1.0/24)
    NETWORK_GATEWAY  gateway for the test network (default: 192.168.1.1)
    NETWORK_IP       static IP for the test VM    (default: 192.168.1.250)
    NETWORK_BRIDGE   PVE bridge override          (default: from CPI_CONFIG)
    NETWORK_DNS      JSON array of DNS servers    (default: ["8.8.8.8"])
    VM_CORES         cores                        (default: 1)
    VM_MEMORY_MIB    memory in MiB                (default: 1024)
    DISK_SIZE_MIB    persistent disk size in MiB  (default: 1024)
    STEMCELL_NAME    override stemcell name       (default: from stemcell.MF)
    STEMCELL_VERSION override stemcell version    (default: from stemcell.MF)
    TRACE            non-empty enables tracing    (default: unset)

Network-test environment (exercises create_network / delete_network):
    NETWORK_TEST_MODE  off | sdn | bridge          (default: off)
    SDN_ZONE           SDN zone name               (mode=sdn; must pre-exist
                       unless the CPI config enables sdn_auto_manage_zone)
    SDN_ZONE_TYPE      zone type for auto-create    (default: empty — omit
                       from cloud_properties so the CPI default, vxlan, governs)
    SDN_VNET           vnet name, 1-8 [a-z0-9]      (mode=sdn, required)
    SDN_RANGE          CIDR for the SDN subnet      (mode=sdn)
    SDN_GATEWAY        gateway for the SDN subnet   (mode=sdn)
    SDN_IP             test VM IP within SDN_RANGE  (mode=sdn)
    BRIDGE_TEST_IFACE  bridge iface to create       (mode=bridge, e.g. vmbr9)

Snapshot/detach ordering (exercises detach_disk's snapshot guard):
    SNAPSHOT_DETACH_MODE  clean | bypass             (default: clean)
                       clean  — delete the snapshot before detaching (PVE rejects
                                detaching a disk a snapshot references; this is
                                the safe order, so detach_disk's guard never fires)
                       bypass — detach while the snapshot is still present; relies
                                on the CPI config setting
                                allow_disk_ops_with_snapshots=true to skip the
                                guard (the test harness writes that config for the
                                bypass pass)

Parked-disk strategy (exercises pve.detached_disk_strategy end to end):
    PARKED_DISK_TEST    on | off                   (default: on)
                       Runs steps 11c-11l, which drive a detached disk through
                       both strategies against live PVE and verify the cluster
                       state after each transition. The pass pins the strategy
                       per call in derived configs, so it proves the same thing
                       whatever CPI_CONFIG happens to set.
    PARKER_RANGE_START  first parker VMID   (default: the band CPI_CONFIG uses)
    PARKER_RANGE_END    last parker VMID    (default: the band CPI_CONFIG uses)
                       These may only restate the band CPI_CONFIG already sets.
                       A value that disagrees aborts the run: the pass would
                       otherwise look for parkers outside the band the CPI parks
                       into and read a real parker as an ordinary VM. A lab that
                       already has guests in 90000-90999 moves the band in the
                       CPI config, which scripts/test integration does from
                       tier1.parked_disk.range_start/range_end; these env vars
                       then restate it.

The pass leaves its parker VM behind, which is the design working as intended:
a parker is durable infrastructure that outlives any one disk, and the CPI has
no call that removes one. Later runs reuse it. To remove it by hand, clear the
protection flag first, or PVE refuses:

    qm set <parker-vmid> --protection 0 && qm destroy <parker-vmid> --purge
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import json
import os
import random
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tarfile
import tempfile
import time
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any

# Import the sibling out-of-band PVE verifier (scripts/_pve_verify.py).
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
import _pve_verify  # noqa: E402


def env(name: str, default: str | None = None, *, required: bool = False) -> str:
    val = os.environ.get(name, default)
    if required and not val:
        sys.exit(f"{name} required")
    return val or ""


def bare_disk_volid(disk_cid: str) -> str:
    """Return disk_cid's bare '<storage>:<volid>' form for CID comparison.

    create_disk/attach_disk emit only 'pvd-' or 'pvz-' envelope CIDs (see
    internal/pve/disk.go); get_disks may echo that exact envelope back or
    fall back to the bare volid it read from PVE config. Decode via the
    already-validated envelope codec (_pve_verify._bare_disk_cid) when an
    envelope prefix is present; anything else is assumed already bare (the
    get_disks fallback path, or a value this harness never produces itself).
    """
    if disk_cid.startswith("pvd-") or disk_cid.startswith("pvz-"):
        return _pve_verify._bare_disk_cid(disk_cid)
    return disk_cid


CPI_CONFIG = env("CPI_CONFIG", required=True)
STEMCELL_PATH = env("STEMCELL_PATH", required=True)
CPI_BIN = env("CPI_BIN", "./bin/cpi")
AGENT_ID = env("AGENT_ID", f"lifecycle-{os.getpid()}")
NETWORK_RANGE = env("NETWORK_RANGE", "192.168.1.0/24")
NETWORK_GATEWAY = env("NETWORK_GATEWAY", "192.168.1.1")
NETWORK_IP = env("NETWORK_IP", "192.168.1.250")
_network_dns_raw = env("NETWORK_DNS", '["8.8.8.8"]')
try:
    NETWORK_DNS: list[str] = json.loads(_network_dns_raw)
    if not isinstance(NETWORK_DNS, list):
        raise ValueError("expected a JSON array")
except (json.JSONDecodeError, ValueError) as _dns_exc:
    sys.exit(
        f"NETWORK_DNS is malformed (got {_network_dns_raw!r}): {_dns_exc}\n"
        'NETWORK_DNS must be a JSON array, e.g. \'["8.8.8.8"]\''
    )
NETWORK_BRIDGE = env("NETWORK_BRIDGE", "")
VM_CORES = int(env("VM_CORES", "1"))
VM_MEMORY_MIB = int(env("VM_MEMORY_MIB", "1024"))
DISK_SIZE_MIB = int(env("DISK_SIZE_MIB", "1024"))
STEMCELL_NAME = env("STEMCELL_NAME", "")
STEMCELL_VERSION = env("STEMCELL_VERSION", "")
TRACE = bool(env("TRACE", ""))

NETWORK_TEST_MODE = env("NETWORK_TEST_MODE", "off").strip().lower()
if NETWORK_TEST_MODE not in ("off", "sdn", "bridge"):
    sys.exit(f"NETWORK_TEST_MODE must be off|sdn|bridge, got {NETWORK_TEST_MODE!r}")
SDN_ZONE = env("SDN_ZONE", "")
SDN_ZONE_TYPE = env("SDN_ZONE_TYPE", "")
SDN_VNET = env("SDN_VNET", "")
SDN_RANGE = env("SDN_RANGE", "")
SDN_GATEWAY = env("SDN_GATEWAY", "")
SDN_IP = env("SDN_IP", "")
BRIDGE_TEST_IFACE = env("BRIDGE_TEST_IFACE", "")

SNAPSHOT_DETACH_MODE = env("SNAPSHOT_DETACH_MODE", "clean").strip().lower()
if SNAPSHOT_DETACH_MODE not in ("clean", "bypass"):
    sys.exit(f"SNAPSHOT_DETACH_MODE must be clean|bypass, got {SNAPSHOT_DETACH_MODE!r}")

if not os.access(CPI_BIN, os.X_OK):
    sys.exit(f"CPI_BIN not executable: {CPI_BIN}")
if not os.access(CPI_CONFIG, os.R_OK):
    sys.exit(f"CPI_CONFIG not readable: {CPI_CONFIG}")
if not os.access(STEMCELL_PATH, os.R_OK):
    sys.exit(f"STEMCELL_PATH not readable: {STEMCELL_PATH}")

PARKED_DISK_TEST = env("PARKED_DISK_TEST", "on").strip().lower()
if PARKED_DISK_TEST not in ("on", "off"):
    sys.exit(f"PARKED_DISK_TEST must be on|off, got {PARKED_DISK_TEST!r}")

# The band comes from CPI_CONFIG, falling back to the CPI's own default. Looking
# for parkers in a band other than the one the base config uses is actively
# dangerous rather than merely wrong: step 11 detaches under CPI_CONFIG and may
# park the disk in the config's band, and a pass looking elsewhere would read
# that parker as an ordinary VM and treat the disk as free.
#
# PARKER_RANGE_START/END therefore only confirm what the base config already
# says. Setting them to anything else is refused rather than honored, because
# honoring them would recreate exactly the mismatch this comment describes.
try:
    with open(CPI_CONFIG) as _cfg_f:
        _base_cfg = json.load(_cfg_f)
except (OSError, ValueError) as _cfg_exc:
    sys.exit(f"CPI_CONFIG is not readable JSON: {_cfg_exc}")
if not isinstance(_base_cfg, dict):
    sys.exit(f"CPI_CONFIG must contain a JSON object, got {type(_base_cfg).__name__}")

_CFG_PARKER_START = int(_base_cfg.get("parked_disk_vmid_range_start") or 90000)
_CFG_PARKER_END = int(_base_cfg.get("parked_disk_vmid_range_end") or 90999)
try:
    PARKER_RANGE_START = int(env("PARKER_RANGE_START", str(_CFG_PARKER_START)))
    PARKER_RANGE_END = int(env("PARKER_RANGE_END", str(_CFG_PARKER_END)))
except ValueError as _parker_exc:
    sys.exit(f"PARKER_RANGE_START/PARKER_RANGE_END must be integers: {_parker_exc}")
if (PARKER_RANGE_START, PARKER_RANGE_END) != (_CFG_PARKER_START, _CFG_PARKER_END):
    sys.exit(
        f"parker range [{PARKER_RANGE_START}, {PARKER_RANGE_END}] does not match the band "
        f"CPI_CONFIG uses [{_CFG_PARKER_START}, {_CFG_PARKER_END}]; move the band in "
        f"{CPI_CONFIG} instead, or the pass would look for parkers where the CPI never puts them"
    )
if PARKER_RANGE_START < 100 or PARKER_RANGE_END <= PARKER_RANGE_START:
    sys.exit(
        f"parker range [{PARKER_RANGE_START}, {PARKER_RANGE_END}] is not a valid VMID band "
        "(start must be >=100 and below end)"
    )

# PVE rejects VMIDs above this ceiling, and the CPI's config validation
# enforces it on the parker band, so a derived band crossing it would fail
# config load mid-pass with an error naming a temp file instead of the cause.
_PVE_MAX_VMID = 999999999
# The band the CPI resolves when both parked_disk_vmid_range keys are absent.
_BUILTIN_PARKER_BAND = (90000, 90999)

# The stranded-parker steps (11g-bis/11h/11h-bis) need a second band that
# cannot contain the lab's parker. Prefer the 1000-VMID window just above the
# lab band; when the lab band ends too close to the VMID ceiling, fall back to
# the window just below it. Computed here so a lab band that fits neither side
# aborts with the cause before the run touches the cluster.
if PARKER_RANGE_END + 1000 <= _PVE_MAX_VMID:
    STRANDED_BAND_START, STRANDED_BAND_END = PARKER_RANGE_END + 1, PARKER_RANGE_END + 1000
elif PARKER_RANGE_START - 1000 >= 100:
    STRANDED_BAND_START, STRANDED_BAND_END = PARKER_RANGE_START - 1000, PARKER_RANGE_START - 1
else:
    sys.exit(
        "cannot derive a moved parker band for the stranded-parker steps (11g-bis/11h/11h-bis): "
        f"the lab band [{PARKER_RANGE_START}, {PARKER_RANGE_END}] leaves no free 1000-VMID window "
        f"above it (that would cross PVE's maximum VMID {_PVE_MAX_VMID}) or below it (that would "
        f"go under VMID 100); shrink or move parked_disk_vmid_range_start/_end in {CPI_CONFIG} "
        "so at least 1000 VMIDs stay clear on one side of the band"
    )

if not NETWORK_BRIDGE:
    NETWORK_BRIDGE = _base_cfg.get("network_bridge", "vmbr0")

_SDN_VNET_RE = re.compile(r'^[a-z0-9]{1,8}$')

# create_network failures naming an unsupported/unknown zone type are an
# environment limitation (old PVE release, missing SDN plugin), not a CPI
# defect — the network pass classifies them as SKIP instead of failing.
_UNSUPPORTED_ZONE_RE = re.compile(
    r"(?i)(?:unsupported|unknown|invalid)[^\n]{0,60}(?:zone[ _-]?type|type[^\n]{0,20}zone)"
    r"|zone[ _-]?type[^\n]{0,60}(?:unsupported|unknown|not supported|invalid)"
)


def validate_sdn_vnet(name: str) -> None:
    """Validate SDN_VNET against PVE vnet naming rules: 1-8 lowercase alphanumeric chars.

    Raises SystemExit with a clear message if invalid — called before any cluster mutation.
    """
    if not name:
        sys.exit("SDN_VNET is empty; provide a 1-8 character lowercase alphanumeric vnet name")
    if not _SDN_VNET_RE.match(name):
        sys.exit(
            f"SDN_VNET {name!r} is invalid — must be 1-8 characters, [a-z0-9] only "
            f"(PVE vnet naming constraint)"
        )


# Validate mode-specific inputs up front so a misconfigured run fails before
# touching the cluster.
if NETWORK_TEST_MODE == "sdn":
    _missing = [
        n
        for n, val in (
            ("SDN_ZONE", SDN_ZONE),
            ("SDN_VNET", SDN_VNET),
            ("SDN_RANGE", SDN_RANGE),
            ("SDN_GATEWAY", SDN_GATEWAY),
            ("SDN_IP", SDN_IP),
        )
        if not val
    ]
    if _missing:
        sys.exit(f"NETWORK_TEST_MODE=sdn requires: {', '.join(_missing)}")
    validate_sdn_vnet(SDN_VNET)
elif NETWORK_TEST_MODE == "bridge":
    if not BRIDGE_TEST_IFACE:
        sys.exit("NETWORK_TEST_MODE=bridge requires BRIDGE_TEST_IFACE")


state: dict[str, Any] = {
    "stemcell_cid": "",
    "vm_cid": "",
    "disk_cid": "",
    "snapshot_cid": "",
    "network_cid": "",
    "disk_attached": False,
    # Config that teardown must use for the disk. The parked pass sets it to a
    # parked-pinned config: once a disk is on a parker, delete must run with
    # the band the parker lives in, and pinning the config keeps teardown
    # independent of whatever band the lab config names.
    "disk_cleanup_cfg": "",
}


def stemcell_cloud_props() -> dict[str, Any]:
    """Build create_stemcell cloud_properties.

    Mirrors a real BOSH director: the director downloads/extracts the
    stemcell tarball and passes the CPI a local image path plus the
    cloud_properties from stemcell.MF. Here there is no director, so read
    name and version from the tarball's stemcell.MF (the CPI requires both
    for direct-qcow upload). STEMCELL_NAME / STEMCELL_VERSION override.
    """
    name, version = STEMCELL_NAME, STEMCELL_VERSION
    if not name or not version:
        try:
            with tarfile.open(STEMCELL_PATH, "r:*") as tar:
                member = tar.extractfile("stemcell.MF")
                text = member.read().decode() if member else ""
        except (KeyError, OSError, tarfile.TarError) as exc:
            sys.exit(f"cannot read stemcell.MF from {STEMCELL_PATH}: {exc}")
        for line in text.splitlines():
            m = re.match(r"(name|version)\s*:\s*'?\"?([^'\"]+?)'?\"?\s*$", line)
            if not m:
                continue
            if m.group(1) == "name" and not name:
                name = m.group(2).strip()
            elif m.group(1) == "version" and not version:
                version = m.group(2).strip()
    if not name or not version:
        sys.exit(
            "stemcell.MF missing name/version; set STEMCELL_NAME and "
            "STEMCELL_VERSION env vars"
        )
    return {"name": name, "version": version}


def extract_stemcell_image() -> tuple[str, "Callable[[], None]"]:
    """Extract the stemcell tarball and return (image_path, cleanup).

    A real BOSH director unpacks the stemcell .tgz and hands the CPI the inner
    `image` file (itself a gzipped tar of root.img), not the outer tarball. The
    CPI's resolveStemcellImage expects that inner file; given the outer .tgz it
    would upload the gzip `image` blob verbatim ("not in qcow2 format"). Mirror
    the director: extract `image` to a temp dir and pass its path.
    """
    tmpdir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="lifecycle-stemcell-")

    def cleanup() -> None:
        shutil.rmtree(tmpdir, ignore_errors=True)

    image_path = os.path.join(tmpdir, "image")
    try:
        with tarfile.open(STEMCELL_PATH, "r:*") as tar:
            member = tar.extractfile("image")
            if member is None:
                raise KeyError("image")
            with open(image_path, "wb") as out:
                shutil.copyfileobj(member, out)
    except (KeyError, OSError, tarfile.TarError) as exc:
        cleanup()
        sys.exit(f"cannot extract 'image' from {STEMCELL_PATH}: {exc}")
    return image_path, cleanup


def step(msg: str) -> None:
    print()
    print(f"==> {msg}")
    print()


# The CPI marks transient failures with ok_to_retry=true and the Director
# retries them with a delay; a client that gives up on the first transient
# (a PVE config-lock timeout during reboot cleanup, for example) fails runs
# the Director would have completed. call() honors the same contract.
RETRIABLE_ATTEMPTS = 4
RETRIABLE_DELAY_SEC = 15


def call(method: str, args: list[Any], config_path: str = "") -> Any:
    for attempt in range(1, RETRIABLE_ATTEMPTS + 1):
        try:
            return _call_once(method, args, config_path)
        except RetriableCPIError as exc:
            if attempt == RETRIABLE_ATTEMPTS:
                raise RuntimeError(str(exc)) from exc
            print(
                f"call {method} retriable failure (attempt {attempt}/{RETRIABLE_ATTEMPTS}), "
                f"retrying in {RETRIABLE_DELAY_SEC}s",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            time.sleep(RETRIABLE_DELAY_SEC)
    raise AssertionError("unreachable")


class RetriableCPIError(RuntimeError):
    """A CPI error envelope with ok_to_retry=true."""


def _call_once(method: str, args: list[Any], config_path: str = "") -> Any:
    req_id = f"{time.time_ns()}-{random.randint(0, 1 << 32)}"
    req = {
        "method": method,
        "arguments": args,
        "context": {"request_id": req_id},
        "api_version": 2,
    }
    if TRACE:
        print(f"  -> {json.dumps(req)}", file=sys.stderr)

    proc = subprocess.run(
        [CPI_BIN, "--config", config_path or CPI_CONFIG],
        input=json.dumps(req) + "\n",
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
    )

    if proc.returncode != 0:
        stderr_snippet = (proc.stderr or "").strip()[-1000:]
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"call {method}: cpi binary exited {proc.returncode}"
            + (f"\nstderr: {stderr_snippet}" if stderr_snippet else "")
        )

    if not proc.stdout.strip():
        raise RuntimeError(f"call {method}: empty response from cpi binary")

    resp = json.loads(proc.stdout)
    if TRACE:
        print(f"  <- {json.dumps(resp)}", file=sys.stderr)

    if resp.get("error") is not None:
        print(f"call {method} failed:", file=sys.stderr)
        print(json.dumps(resp["error"], indent=2), file=sys.stderr)
        err_msg = ""
        retriable = False
        if isinstance(resp["error"], dict):
            err_msg = str(resp["error"].get("message", "")).strip()
            retriable = resp["error"].get("ok_to_retry") is True
        full_msg = f"{method} failed: {err_msg}" if err_msg else f"{method} failed"
        if retriable:
            raise RetriableCPIError(full_msg)
        raise RuntimeError(full_msg)

    return resp.get("result")


# Temp CPI configs derived from CPI_CONFIG, removed once teardown is done.
# They outlive the step that created them because cleanup() may still need one:
# a disk left on a parker can only be deleted by a call whose config knows about
# parking.
_derived_configs: list[str] = []


def derive_config(prefix: str, **overrides: Any) -> str:
    """Write a temp CPI config that is CPI_CONFIG plus overrides.

    An override of None REMOVES the key instead of setting it, so a derived
    config can also exercise what the CPI does when a property is absent.
    Lets the harness exercise config-selected code paths without mutating the
    primary config, and without depending on what the primary config happens to
    say. Removal is handled centrally by purge_derived_configs().
    """
    with open(CPI_CONFIG) as f:
        cfg = json.load(f)
    for key, value in overrides.items():
        if value is None:
            cfg.pop(key, None)
        else:
            cfg[key] = value
    fd, path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=prefix, suffix=".json")
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as f:
        json.dump(cfg, f)
    _derived_configs.append(path)
    return path


def purge_derived_configs() -> None:
    """Remove every derived config. Call once teardown can no longer need one."""
    for path in _derived_configs:
        Path(path).unlink(missing_ok=True)
    _derived_configs.clear()


def write_reboot_config(mode: str) -> str:
    """Derive a temp CPI config from CPI_CONFIG with reboot_mode overridden."""
    return derive_config("cpi-reboot-", reboot_mode=mode)


def write_strategy_config(strategy: str) -> str:
    """Derive a temp CPI config pinning detached_disk_strategy and the parker band.

    Both keys are pinned so the pass always operates on the band the parker
    was created in, whatever the lab config names — the band resolves under
    every strategy, so pinning it is about determinism across labs, not
    reachability. The strategy is the only semantic switch: under "free"
    parking stops but disks parked earlier stay reachable.
    write_stranded_parker_config covers the unsupported shape, the band moved
    away from a live parker.
    """
    return derive_config(
        f"cpi-{strategy}-",
        detached_disk_strategy=strategy,
        parked_disk_vmid_range_start=PARKER_RANGE_START,
        parked_disk_vmid_range_end=PARKER_RANGE_END,
    )


def write_stranded_parker_config() -> str:
    """Derive a temp CPI config that opts out of parking AND moves the band.

    This is the shape an operator lands in by pointing the parker band at a
    window away from where parker VMs already live. (Dropping the band no
    longer strands anything: an unset band resolves to the built-in one under
    every strategy, so the parker stays recognized and the disk drains.) With
    the band moved, the CPI can no longer recognize a parker, so a disk still
    sitting on one looks like a volume attached to an ordinary VM. The harness
    uses it to prove attach_disk refuses that rather than writing a second
    reference to the same volume.

    The moved band is the 1000-VMID window next to the lab band — above it
    normally, below it when the lab band ends too close to PVE's maximum VMID
    (the CPI's config validation rejects a band crossing the ceiling). The
    window is chosen and validated at startup, so a band it cannot fit around
    aborts the run before anything touches the cluster.
    """
    return derive_config(
        "cpi-stranded-",
        detached_disk_strategy="free",
        parked_disk_vmid_range_start=STRANDED_BAND_START,
        parked_disk_vmid_range_end=STRANDED_BAND_END,
    )


def write_unset_band_config() -> str:
    """Derive a temp CPI config with strategy=free and the band keys REMOVED.

    This is the shape an operator lands in by reverting to "free" and deleting
    the band properties outright: the CPI must resolve the built-in
    90000-90999 band on its own so a disk parked while "parked" was in effect
    still drains. Only meaningful on a lab whose configured band IS the
    built-in one — anywhere else the resolved band cannot contain the lab's
    parker, which is the stranded shape write_stranded_parker_config covers.
    """
    return derive_config(
        "cpi-unsetband-",
        detached_disk_strategy="free",
        parked_disk_vmid_range_start=None,
        parked_disk_vmid_range_end=None,
    )


def build_network_spec() -> dict[str, Any]:
    """Build the create_network network_spec for the active NETWORK_TEST_MODE.

    SDN mode supplies cloud_properties.zone+vnet (routes the handler to the SDN
    path). zone_type is included only when SDN_ZONE_TYPE is set; omitted, the
    CPI's default zone type (vxlan) governs. Bridge mode supplies
    cloud_properties.bridge and lets the handler default the target node to
    config.node.
    """
    if NETWORK_TEST_MODE == "sdn":
        cloud_props: dict[str, Any] = {"zone": SDN_ZONE, "vnet": SDN_VNET}
        if SDN_ZONE_TYPE:
            cloud_props["zone_type"] = SDN_ZONE_TYPE
        return {
            "type": "manual",
            "range": SDN_RANGE,
            "gateway": SDN_GATEWAY,
            "cloud_properties": cloud_props,
        }
    return {
        "type": "manual",
        "range": NETWORK_RANGE,
        "gateway": NETWORK_GATEWAY,
        "cloud_properties": {"bridge": BRIDGE_TEST_IFACE},
    }


def run_parked_disk_pass(verifier: "_pve_verify.PVEVerifier") -> int:
    """Drive a detached disk through both detached-disk strategies against live PVE.

    Entered after step 11 with the disk detached and the VM still running. What
    the base config did with that disk is deliberately not assumed: the pass
    re-attaches first, then drives every transition with a strategy pinned per
    call, so it proves the same thing on a lab whose CPI_CONFIG sets parked,
    free, or nothing at all.

    Every claim is checked against the cluster rather than against the CPI's
    answer, because parking is invisible in the JSON-RPC results — detach_disk
    returns null whether it parked the disk or left it floating.

    Paths covered:

      detach (parked)
        the disk lands on a parker VM: inside the band, tagged bosh-parker,
        protected, set not to start with the node, and named in the parker's
        provenance sentinel

      has_disk (parked)
        a parked disk still exists

      set_disk_metadata (parked)
        the parker is skipped rather than written to

      snapshot_disk (parked)
        refused, because a PVE snapshot takes the whole parker and with it
        every other deployment's parked disk

      snapshot_disk (free, band moved)
        still refused, because the holder is classified by its bosh-parker tag
        rather than by the band the config happens to name

      attach (free, band moved)
        refused, because a parker the config can no longer recognize still
        holds the volume and attaching it again would double-reference it

      attach (parked)
        the unpark path: protection is cleared for the detach and put back
        afterwards, and the slot and provenance entry are released

      detach (free)
        the opt-out leaves the volume free-floating, with no parker holding it

      detach (parked) again
        the existing parker is reused instead of a second one being created

      attach (free, band unset)
        the self-drain path an operator lands in by reverting to "free" and
        deleting the band properties: the CPI resolves the built-in band on
        its own, still recognizes the parker, and drains the disk — run only
        on labs whose band is the built-in one, announced as a SKIP with the
        reason anywhere else

      delete (free, band moved)
        refused for the same reason the attach is, because freeing the volume
        would leave the parker's slot pointing at storage that is gone

      delete_vm on the parker
        refused, because a parker may still hold other deployments' disks

    Returns the parker VMID so the caller can assert it survives delete_vm.
    """
    parked_cfg = write_strategy_config("parked")
    # Kept separately from disk_cleanup_cfg, which teardown clears once the disk
    # is gone. Step 15a still needs a config that pins the parked strategy: run
    # under the lab's base config instead and the step proves whatever that
    # config happens to say, which for a lab configured "free" is nothing at
    # all.
    state["parked_cfg"] = parked_cfg
    free_cfg = write_strategy_config("free")
    stranded_cfg = write_stranded_parker_config()
    # From here on the disk may be parked, so teardown has to speak parked too.
    state["disk_cleanup_cfg"] = parked_cfg

    vm_cid, disk_cid = state["vm_cid"], state["disk_cid"]
    bare_volid = bare_disk_volid(disk_cid)
    parkers_before = verifier.parker_vmids(PARKER_RANGE_START, PARKER_RANGE_END)
    print(f"    parkers already on the node: {parkers_before or '(none)'}")

    step("11c. attach_disk (strategy=parked; unparks the disk if step 11 parked it)")
    call("attach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=parked_cfg)
    state["disk_attached"] = True
    print("attach_disk=ok")

    step("11d. detach_disk (strategy=parked)")
    call("detach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=parked_cfg)
    state["disk_attached"] = False
    print("detach_disk=ok")

    holders = verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid)
    if len(holders) != 1:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"expected exactly one VM to hold {bare_volid} after a parked detach, got {holders}"
        )
    parker_vmid, slot = holders[0]
    parkers = verifier.parker_vmids(PARKER_RANGE_START, PARKER_RANGE_END)
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"disk held by parker {parker_vmid} at slot {slot}", parker_vmid in parkers
    )
    if not slot.startswith("scsi"):
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"parked disk landed in {slot!r}; the parked strategy attaches to scsi0-scsi30"
        )
    if len(parkers) > len(parkers_before) + 1:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"parking one disk created more than one parker: {parkers_before} -> {parkers}"
        )

    parker_cfg = verifier.qemu_config(parker_vmid)
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} is named bosh-parker-{parker_vmid}",
        str(parker_cfg.get("name", "")) == f"bosh-parker-{parker_vmid}",
    )
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} is protected against disk and VM removal",
        str(parker_cfg.get("protection", "0")) == "1",
    )
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} does not start with the node",
        str(parker_cfg.get("onboot", "0")) == "0",
    )
    # The CPI writes provenance best-effort and never fails a park over it, so
    # nothing in production surfaces a broken sentinel. This harness is the only
    # place it can surface, which is why the assertion is hard here.
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} records the disk in its provenance sentinel",
        bare_volid in verifier.parked_disks(parker_vmid),
    )
    parker_tags = str(parker_cfg.get("tags", ""))
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} carries the CPI ownership tag",
        any(t.strip().lower() == "bosh-cpi" for t in parker_tags.split(";")),
    )

    step("11d-bis. detach_disk again (parked: the already-parked path is idempotent)")
    call("detach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=parked_cfg)
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"the second detach left the disk on parker {parker_vmid} at {slot}",
        verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid) == [(parker_vmid, slot)],
    )

    # has_disk reads storage content and has no parker logic of its own, so this
    # proves the volume survived the park rather than anything about parking.
    # That is still worth asserting: a park that moved or renamed the volume
    # would make the Director believe the disk is gone.
    step("11e. has_disk (the volume survives the park)")
    if call("has_disk", [disk_cid], config_path=parked_cfg) is not True:
        raise RuntimeError("has_disk returned false for a parked disk")
    print("has_disk=true")

    step("11f. set_disk_metadata (parked: the parker must be skipped, not written)")
    desc_before = str(verifier.qemu_config(parker_vmid).get("description", ""))
    call(
        "set_disk_metadata",
        [disk_cid, {"deployment": "lifecycle-test", "director": "local-harness"}],
        config_path=parked_cfg,
    )
    desc_after = str(verifier.qemu_config(parker_vmid).get("description", ""))
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} description untouched by set_disk_metadata",
        desc_after == desc_before,
    )

    step("11g. snapshot_disk (parked: must be refused)")
    try:
        snap_cid = call("snapshot_disk", [disk_cid, {}], config_path=parked_cfg)
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        if "held by a parker vm" not in str(exc).lower():
            raise RuntimeError(
                f"snapshot_disk on a parked disk failed for the wrong reason: {exc}"
            ) from exc
        print("snapshot_disk refused the parked disk as expected")
    else:
        # It succeeded, so a snapshot of the whole parker now exists. Record it
        # for teardown before failing, or the run leaks it.
        state["snapshot_cid"] = snap_cid
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"snapshot_disk snapshotted parker {parker_vmid} (snapshot_cid={snap_cid}); "
            "the parker guard did not fire"
        )

    step("11g-bis. snapshot_disk (strategy=free with the band moved: still refused)")
    # The guard classifies the holder by its bosh-parker tag, not by whether its
    # VMID sits in a configured band, so moving the band must not open a path
    # to snapshotting the parker.
    try:
        snap_cid = call("snapshot_disk", [disk_cid, {}], config_path=stranded_cfg)
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        if "held by a parker vm" not in str(exc).lower():
            raise RuntimeError(
                f"snapshot_disk on a stranded parker failed for the wrong reason: {exc}"
            ) from exc
        print("snapshot_disk refused the stranded parked disk as expected")
    else:
        state["snapshot_cid"] = snap_cid
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"snapshot_disk snapshotted parker {parker_vmid} with the band moved "
            f"(snapshot_cid={snap_cid}); the parker guard is still band-gated"
        )

    step("11h. attach_disk (strategy=free with the band moved: the stranded-parker refusal)")
    try:
        call("attach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=stranded_cfg)
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        if "bosh-parker" not in str(exc):
            raise RuntimeError(
                f"attach_disk refused the stranded parked disk for the wrong reason: {exc}"
            ) from exc
        print("attach_disk refused the disk and named the parker as expected")
    else:
        # The attach went through, so the volume is now referenced by both the
        # parker and the VM. Record the attachment so teardown detaches it, then
        # fail: this is the double-reference the guard exists to prevent.
        state["disk_attached"] = True
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"attach_disk attached a disk parker {parker_vmid} still holds; "
            f"holders are now {verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid)}"
        )
    holders = verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid)
    if [h[0] for h in holders] != [parker_vmid]:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"the refused attach should have left the disk on parker {parker_vmid}, got {holders}"
        )

    step("11h-bis. delete_disk (strategy=free with the band moved: the stranded-parker refusal)")
    try:
        call("delete_disk", [disk_cid], config_path=stranded_cfg)
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        if "bosh-parker" not in str(exc):
            raise RuntimeError(
                f"delete_disk refused the stranded parked disk for the wrong reason: {exc}"
            ) from exc
        print("delete_disk refused the disk and named the parker as expected")
    else:
        # The volume is gone while the parker's slot still references it. Nothing
        # downstream can recover from that, so say exactly what happened.
        state["disk_cid"] = ""
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"delete_disk freed a volume parker {parker_vmid} still holds; "
            "the parker's slot now references storage that no longer exists"
        )
    verifier.assert_true(
        "the refused delete left the volume in place", verifier.volume_exists(disk_cid)
    )

    step("11i. attach_disk (strategy=parked: unpark, which clears and restores protection)")
    call("attach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=parked_cfg)
    state["disk_attached"] = True
    holders = verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid)
    if [str(h[0]) for h in holders] != [str(vm_cid).strip()]:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"after unpark the disk should be held only by VM {vm_cid}, got {holders}"
        )
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} protection restored after the unpark detach",
        str(verifier.qemu_config(parker_vmid).get("protection", "0")) == "1",
    )
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} dropped the disk from its provenance sentinel",
        bare_volid not in verifier.parked_disks(parker_vmid),
    )

    step("11j. detach_disk (strategy=free: the opt-out leaves the volume free-floating)")
    call("detach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=free_cfg)
    state["disk_attached"] = False
    verifier.assert_true(
        "no VM holds the disk under strategy=free", verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid) == []
    )
    verifier.assert_true(
        "the free-floating volume still exists", verifier.volume_exists(disk_cid)
    )

    step("11k. attach_disk (strategy=free: a free-floating disk re-attaches)")
    call("attach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=free_cfg)
    state["disk_attached"] = True
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"VM {vm_cid} is the sole holder after the free re-attach",
        [str(h[0]) for h in verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid)] == [str(vm_cid).strip()],
    )

    step("11l. detach_disk (strategy=parked: the existing parker is reused)")
    call("detach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=parked_cfg)
    state["disk_attached"] = False
    holders = verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid)
    if [h[0] for h in holders] != [parker_vmid]:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"re-parking should have returned the disk to parker {parker_vmid}, got {holders}"
        )
    verifier.assert_true(
        "no second parker VM was created",
        verifier.parker_vmids(PARKER_RANGE_START, PARKER_RANGE_END) == parkers,
    )

    step("11l-bis. attach_disk (strategy=free, band keys removed: the built-in band still drains)")
    if (PARKER_RANGE_START, PARKER_RANGE_END) != _BUILTIN_PARKER_BAND:
        print(
            f"==> SKIP: this lab's parker band [{PARKER_RANGE_START}, {PARKER_RANGE_END}] is not "
            f"the built-in {_BUILTIN_PARKER_BAND[0]}-{_BUILTIN_PARKER_BAND[1]}. With the band "
            "keys removed the CPI resolves the built-in band, which cannot contain this lab's "
            "parker — that stranded shape is already proven by steps 11g-bis/11h/11h-bis. To run "
            f"this step live, point parked_disk_vmid_range_start/_end in {CPI_CONFIG} at the "
            "built-in band."
        )
        return parker_vmid

    unset_band_cfg = write_unset_band_config()
    try:
        call("attach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=unset_band_cfg)
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "attach_disk with detached_disk_strategy=free and no parked_disk_vmid_range keys "
            f"failed to drain the disk off parker {parker_vmid}: {exc}\n"
            "An unset band must resolve to the built-in 90000-90999 under every strategy "
            '(ApplyDefaults in internal/config); an operator who reverts to "free" and deletes '
            "the band properties would otherwise strand every previously parked disk"
        ) from exc
    state["disk_attached"] = True
    holders = verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid)
    if [str(h[0]) for h in holders] != [str(vm_cid).strip()]:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"after the unset-band drain the disk should be held only by VM {vm_cid}, got {holders}"
        )
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} protection restored after the unset-band drain",
        str(verifier.qemu_config(parker_vmid).get("protection", "0")) == "1",
    )
    verifier.assert_true(
        f"parker {parker_vmid} dropped the disk from its provenance sentinel",
        bare_volid not in verifier.parked_disks(parker_vmid),
    )

    step("11l-ter. detach_disk (strategy=parked: re-park so the pass ends where it started)")
    call("detach_disk", [vm_cid, disk_cid], config_path=parked_cfg)
    state["disk_attached"] = False
    holders = verifier.disk_holders(disk_cid)
    if [h[0] for h in holders] != [parker_vmid]:
        raise RuntimeError(
            "re-parking after the unset-band drain should have returned the disk to "
            f"parker {parker_vmid}, got {holders}"
        )

    return parker_vmid


def cleanup() -> None:
    if state["snapshot_cid"]:
        try:
            call("delete_snapshot", [state["snapshot_cid"]])
        except Exception:
            pass
    disk_cfg = state["disk_cleanup_cfg"]
    if state["disk_attached"] and state["vm_cid"] and state["disk_cid"]:
        try:
            call("detach_disk", [state["vm_cid"], state["disk_cid"]], config_path=disk_cfg)
        except Exception:
            pass
    if state["disk_cid"]:
        try:
            call("delete_disk", [state["disk_cid"]], config_path=disk_cfg)
        except Exception:
            pass
    if state["vm_cid"]:
        try:
            call("delete_vm", [state["vm_cid"]])
        except Exception:
            pass
    if state["stemcell_cid"]:
        try:
            call("delete_stemcell", [state["stemcell_cid"]])
        except Exception:
            pass
    if state["network_cid"]:
        try:
            call("delete_network", [state["network_cid"]])
        except Exception:
            pass


def main() -> int:
    try:
        # Out-of-band PVE verifier (same host+auth as the CPI binary). A run
        # that cannot reach PVE to verify must not be able to report success.
        verifier = _pve_verify.PVEVerifier.from_config_file(CPI_CONFIG)

        step("1. info")
        info = call("info", [])
        print(json.dumps(info, indent=2))

        step(f"2. create_stemcell (image={STEMCELL_PATH})")
        image_path, stemcell_cleanup = extract_stemcell_image()
        try:
            state["stemcell_cid"] = call(
                "create_stemcell", [image_path, stemcell_cloud_props()]
            )
        finally:
            stemcell_cleanup()
        print(f"stemcell_cid={state['stemcell_cid']}")

        # Effective create_vm network params. When the network test is off these
        # are today's static values; otherwise create_network supplies the
        # bridge (the realized vnet for SDN, or the created iface for bridge).
        eff_bridge = NETWORK_BRIDGE
        eff_ip = NETWORK_IP
        eff_gateway = NETWORK_GATEWAY

        if NETWORK_TEST_MODE != "off":
            step(f"2a. create_network (mode={NETWORK_TEST_MODE})")
            try:
                net_res = call("create_network", [build_network_spec()])
            except RuntimeError as net_exc:
                # A PVE host that lacks the requested zone type (e.g. an older
                # release without vxlan support) is an environment limitation,
                # not a CPI defect — classify as a skip. Everything else stays
                # fatal.
                if _UNSUPPORTED_ZONE_RE.search(str(net_exc)):
                    print()
                    print(f"==> SKIP: PVE host does not support the requested zone type ({net_exc})")
                    print()
                    cleanup()
                    return 0
                raise
            state["network_cid"] = net_res[0]
            net_cloud_props = net_res[2] if len(net_res) > 2 else {}
            print(f"network_cid={state['network_cid']}")

            if NETWORK_TEST_MODE == "sdn":
                verifier.assert_true(
                    f"sdn vnet {state['network_cid']!r} exists",
                    verifier.vnet_exists(state["network_cid"]),
                )
                verifier.assert_true(
                    f"sdn subnet {SDN_RANGE!r} present on vnet",
                    verifier.subnet_present(state["network_cid"], SDN_RANGE),
                )
                if not verifier.bridge_realized(state["network_cid"]):
                    print(
                        f"    verify: bridge realization for vnet "
                        f"{state['network_cid']!r} not yet visible (non-fatal)"
                    )
                eff_bridge = net_cloud_props.get("bridge", state["network_cid"])
                eff_ip, eff_gateway = SDN_IP, SDN_GATEWAY
            else:  # bridge
                verifier.assert_true(
                    f"bridge {state['network_cid']!r} exists",
                    verifier.bridge_exists(state["network_cid"]),
                )
                eff_bridge = net_cloud_props.get("bridge", state["network_cid"])

        step(f"3. create_vm (agent_id={AGENT_ID}, stemcell_cid={state['stemcell_cid']})")
        cloud_props = {"cores": VM_CORES, "memory": VM_MEMORY_MIB}
        if NETWORK_TEST_MODE == "bridge":
            # A plain Linux bridge exists on ONE node -- create_network built it
            # on the CPI config's node -- while create_vm places by headroom
            # anywhere in the cluster. Pin the VM to the bridge's node, or on a
            # multi-node cluster it starts on a node where the bridge does not
            # exist and qmstart fails. SDN vnets need no pin: the zone realizes
            # them on every node.
            _pin = str(_base_cfg.get("node", "")).strip()
            if _pin:
                cloud_props["target_node"] = _pin
        networks = {
            "default": {
                "type": "manual",
                "ip": eff_ip,
                "netmask": "255.255.255.0",
                "gateway": eff_gateway,
                "dns": NETWORK_DNS,
                "default": ["dns", "gateway"],
                "cloud_properties": {"bridge": eff_bridge},
            }
        }
        create_vm_res = call(
            "create_vm",
            [AGENT_ID, state["stemcell_cid"], cloud_props, networks, [], {}],
        )
        state["vm_cid"] = create_vm_res[0]
        print(f"vm_cid={state['vm_cid']}")
        verifier.assert_true(
            f"vm {state['vm_cid']!r} exists on pve",
            verifier.vm_exists(state["vm_cid"]),
        )

        step("4. has_vm")
        has_vm = call("has_vm", [state["vm_cid"]])
        if has_vm is not True:
            raise RuntimeError(f"has_vm returned {has_vm}, expected true")
        print("has_vm=true")

        step("5. set_vm_metadata")
        call(
            "set_vm_metadata",
            [state["vm_cid"], {"deployment": "lifecycle-test", "director": "local-harness"}],
        )
        print("set_vm_metadata=ok")

        step(f"6. create_disk (size={DISK_SIZE_MIB} MiB)")
        state["disk_cid"] = call("create_disk", [DISK_SIZE_MIB, {}, state["vm_cid"]])
        print(f"disk_cid={state['disk_cid']}")
        verifier.assert_true(
            f"disk volume {state['disk_cid']!r} exists on pve",
            verifier.volume_exists(state["disk_cid"]),
        )

        # The CID envelope records the format the disk was created under and
        # attach_disk trusts it, so it must match what PVE actually created:
        # on block-native storage (lvm/lvmthin/zfspool/rbd) PVE always
        # allocates raw whatever a file-format default says. Tolerant when
        # either side carries no format (legacy CID, plugin that elides the
        # field); a real mismatch aborts the run like any other verify.
        fmt_result = verifier.check_volume_format(state["disk_cid"])
        print(f"    verify: disk format matches CID envelope = {fmt_result}")

        # DiskCIDMeta.Node is documented "populated for node-local backends;
        # empty for shared storage" — a shared volume is reachable from any
        # node, and a stale pin would steer verification and placement at a
        # node that means nothing. Only assertable when the pool really is a
        # shared type; on local pools the pin is correct and expected.
        _disk_pool = bare_disk_volid(state["disk_cid"]).split(":", 1)[0]
        if verifier.storage_is_shared(_disk_pool):
            verifier.assert_false(
                f"node pin in CID for shared storage {_disk_pool!r}",
                bool(_pve_verify._cid_node(state["disk_cid"])),
            )

        # The parked strategy parks a fresh disk at create, before anything
        # attaches it, so it is never exposed as an unowned free-floating
        # volume. Assert the parker holds it already. Under strategy=free the
        # fresh disk stays free-floating; nothing to assert there.
        base_strategy = str(_base_cfg.get("detached_disk_strategy") or "parked").strip().lower()
        if PARKED_DISK_TEST == "on" and base_strategy == "parked":
            fresh_holders = verifier.disk_holders(state["disk_cid"])
            if len(fresh_holders) != 1:
                raise RuntimeError(
                    "expected exactly one parker to hold the fresh disk after "
                    f"create_disk, got {fresh_holders}"
                )
            fresh_parker, fresh_slot = fresh_holders[0]
            verifier.assert_true(
                f"fresh disk parked on parker {fresh_parker} at {fresh_slot}",
                fresh_parker in verifier.parker_vmids(PARKER_RANGE_START, PARKER_RANGE_END),
            )

        step("6a. has_disk (post-create)")
        hd = call("has_disk", [state["disk_cid"]])
        if hd is not True:
            raise RuntimeError(f"has_disk returned {hd}, expected true")
        print("has_disk=true")

        step("7. attach_disk")
        call("attach_disk", [state["vm_cid"], state["disk_cid"]])
        state["disk_attached"] = True
        print("attach_disk=ok")
        # Re-check the format agreement now that the volume is on a VM bus:
        # attach_disk is the call that consumes the recorded format, so this
        # is the point where a drifted envelope would have just done damage.
        fmt_result = verifier.check_volume_format(state["disk_cid"])
        print(f"    verify: disk format matches CID envelope = {fmt_result}")

        step("7a. set_disk_metadata")
        call("set_disk_metadata", [state["disk_cid"], {
            "deployment": "lifecycle-test",
            "director": "local-harness",
            "instance_id": AGENT_ID,
        }])
        print("set_disk_metadata=ok")

        step(f"7b. resize_disk ({DISK_SIZE_MIB} MiB -> {DISK_SIZE_MIB * 2} MiB)")
        call("resize_disk", [state["disk_cid"], DISK_SIZE_MIB * 2])
        print("resize_disk=ok")

        step("7c. update_disk")
        call("update_disk", [state["disk_cid"], {"cache": "writeback", "iothread": True}])
        print("update_disk=ok")

        step("8. get_disks")
        disks = call("get_disks", [state["vm_cid"]])
        print(f"disks={json.dumps(disks)}")
        # get_disks returns the exact pvd-/pvz- envelope CID attach_disk
        # recorded in the VM's description sentinel when present, falling
        # back to the bare volid otherwise (internal/cpi/handlers/get_disks.go).
        # Decode both sides to the bare volid before comparing so either
        # return shape matches create_disk's envelope CID.
        want_bare = bare_disk_volid(state["disk_cid"])
        got_bare = [bare_disk_volid(d) for d in disks]
        if want_bare not in got_bare:
            raise RuntimeError(f"get_disks did not include {state['disk_cid']}")

        step("9. snapshot_disk")
        state["snapshot_cid"] = call(
            "snapshot_disk", [state["disk_cid"], {"deployment": "lifecycle-test"}]
        )
        print(f"snapshot_cid={state['snapshot_cid']}")

        # Steps 10-11 ordering depends on SNAPSHOT_DETACH_MODE.
        #   clean  — delete the snapshot first, then detach. PVE refuses to detach
        #            a disk a snapshot still references, so the safe teardown order
        #            removes the snapshot before detach; detach_disk's guard never
        #            fires.
        #   bypass — detach while the snapshot is still present, relying on the
        #            CPI's allow_disk_ops_with_snapshots=true (set in this pass's
        #            config) to skip the guard. PVE leaves the volume in an unusedN
        #            slot (its sweep is blocked by the snapshot); after deleting the
        #            snapshot, a retry detach_disk sweeps that slot so delete_disk
        #            can free the volume and delete_vm is unblocked. Exercises the
        #            full documented bypass + recovery path.
        if SNAPSHOT_DETACH_MODE == "bypass":
            step("10. detach_disk (bypass: snapshot present, allow_disk_ops_with_snapshots=true)")
            call("detach_disk", [state["vm_cid"], state["disk_cid"]])
            state["disk_attached"] = False
            print("detach_disk(bypass)=ok")

            step("11. delete_snapshot")
            call("delete_snapshot", [state["snapshot_cid"]])
            state["snapshot_cid"] = ""
            print("delete_snapshot=ok")

            # The bypassed detach parked the disk in an unusedN slot (PVE's sweep
            # was blocked by the snapshot). With the snapshot gone, retry
            # detach_disk to complete the sweep so delete_disk can free the volume
            # and delete_vm is not blocked by a dangling unused slot.
            step("11b. detach_disk (retry: sweep lingering unused slot)")
            call("detach_disk", [state["vm_cid"], state["disk_cid"]])
            print("detach_disk(retry sweep)=ok")
        else:
            step("10. delete_snapshot (clean: before detach)")
            call("delete_snapshot", [state["snapshot_cid"]])
            state["snapshot_cid"] = ""
            print("delete_snapshot=ok")

            step("11. detach_disk")
            call("detach_disk", [state["vm_cid"], state["disk_cid"]])
            state["disk_attached"] = False
            print("detach_disk=ok")

        # Steps 11c-11l: the parked detached-disk strategy, end to end. Left
        # until after the snapshot ordering above so it starts from a plain
        # detached disk with no snapshot in the way.
        parker_vmid = 0
        if PARKED_DISK_TEST == "on":
            parker_vmid = run_parked_disk_pass(verifier)

        # delete_disk runs against whichever config the disk's state requires:
        # after the parked pass the disk is on a parker, and only a call that
        # knows about parking will unpark it before freeing the volume.
        delete_cfg = state["disk_cleanup_cfg"]
        step("12. delete_disk")
        deleted_disk_cid = state["disk_cid"]
        call("delete_disk", [state["disk_cid"]], config_path=delete_cfg)
        step("12a. has_disk (post-delete)")
        hd = call("has_disk", [state["disk_cid"]], config_path=delete_cfg)
        if hd is not False:
            raise RuntimeError(f"has_disk post-delete returned {hd}, expected false")
        print("has_disk=false")
        if parker_vmid:
            verifier.assert_true(
                f"parker {parker_vmid} released the slot on delete_disk",
                verifier.disk_holders(deleted_disk_cid) == [],
            )
            verifier.assert_true(
                f"parker {parker_vmid} dropped the disk from its provenance sentinel",
                bare_disk_volid(deleted_disk_cid) not in verifier.parked_disks(parker_vmid),
            )
        state["disk_cid"] = ""
        state["disk_cleanup_cfg"] = ""
        print("delete_disk=ok")

        # reboot_vm is exercised here, while the VM is still running, before
        # it is torn down. Both config-selectable modes are checked against
        # live PVE: soft issues a graceful ACPI reboot (POST /status/reboot)
        # with a hard-reset fallback; hard issues an immediate reset
        # (POST /status/reset). has_vm must stay true after each — the VM is
        # expected to be running again once reboot_vm returns.
        step("13. reboot_vm (soft graceful reboot, default config)")
        call("reboot_vm", [state["vm_cid"]])
        if call("has_vm", [state["vm_cid"]]) is not True:
            raise RuntimeError("has_vm returned false after soft reboot_vm")
        print("reboot_vm(soft)=ok")

        hard_cfg = write_reboot_config("hard")
        step("14. reboot_vm (hard reset, reboot_mode=hard)")
        call("reboot_vm", [state["vm_cid"]], config_path=hard_cfg)
        if call("has_vm", [state["vm_cid"]]) is not True:
            raise RuntimeError("has_vm returned false after hard reboot_vm")
        print("reboot_vm(hard)=ok")

        step("15. delete_vm")
        deleted_vm_cid = state["vm_cid"]
        call("delete_vm", [state["vm_cid"]])
        state["vm_cid"] = ""
        print("delete_vm=ok")
        verifier.assert_false(
            f"vm {deleted_vm_cid!r} gone from pve",
            verifier.vm_exists(deleted_vm_cid),
        )
        if parker_vmid:
            # delete_vm refuses to destroy a parker: it is shared infrastructure
            # that may still hold other deployments' disks. Hand the parker's own
            # VMID to delete_vm, because that is the only argument the refusal
            # reads. Asserting the parker survived the workload VM's deletion
            # would pass with the guard removed entirely.
            # The assertion is made by ATTEMPTING the destroy, so a regressed
            # guard runs it for real: the fast path issues skiplock + purge,
            # which bypasses protection=1 and takes every disk in the parker's
            # slots. That is acceptable against a parker this run filled and
            # emptied, and not acceptable against one holding somebody else's
            # disks, so check before betting the lab on it.
            foreign = [
                slot
                for slot, volid in verifier.parker_disk_slots(parker_vmid).items()
                if volid
            ]
            if foreign:
                # Skip the destructive assertion, but keep going: steps 16 and
                # 16a still have to run or this run leaks a stemcell and a
                # network. A skip is not a failure.
                print(
                    f"15a. SKIPPED: parker {parker_vmid} holds {len(foreign)} disk(s) "
                    f"({', '.join(sorted(foreign))}) this run did not put there; "
                    "a regressed guard would destroy them"
                )
            else:
                step(f"15a. delete_vm refuses parker {parker_vmid}")
                try:
                    call("delete_vm", [str(parker_vmid)], config_path=state["parked_cfg"])
                except RuntimeError as exc:
                    if "parker" not in str(exc).lower():
                        raise RuntimeError(
                            f"delete_vm on parker {parker_vmid} failed for the wrong reason: {exc}"
                        ) from exc
                    print("delete_vm refused the parker as expected")
                else:
                    raise RuntimeError(
                        f"delete_vm destroyed or accepted parker {parker_vmid}; "
                        "the parker guard did not fire"
                    )
                verifier.assert_true(
                    f"parker {parker_vmid} still present", verifier.vm_exists(parker_vmid)
                )

        step("16. delete_stemcell")
        call("delete_stemcell", [state["stemcell_cid"]])
        state["stemcell_cid"] = ""
        print("delete_stemcell=ok")

        if NETWORK_TEST_MODE != "off" and state["network_cid"]:
            step(f"16a. delete_network (mode={NETWORK_TEST_MODE})")
            deleted_net_cid = state["network_cid"]
            call("delete_network", [state["network_cid"]])
            state["network_cid"] = ""
            print("delete_network=ok")
            if NETWORK_TEST_MODE == "sdn":
                verifier.assert_false(
                    f"sdn vnet {deleted_net_cid!r} gone",
                    verifier.vnet_exists(deleted_net_cid),
                )
            else:  # bridge
                verifier.assert_false(
                    f"bridge {deleted_net_cid!r} gone",
                    verifier.bridge_exists(deleted_net_cid),
                )

        if parker_vmid:
            print()
            print(
                f"==> parker VM {parker_vmid} left in place (durable by design; later runs "
                f"reuse it).\n    Remove it with: qm set {parker_vmid} --protection 0 && "
                f"qm destroy {parker_vmid} --purge"
            )

        print()
        print("==> lifecycle complete")
        print()
        return 0
    except Exception as e:
        print()
        print(f"==> failure detected: {e} — best-effort cleanup", file=sys.stderr)
        print()
        cleanup()
        return 1
    finally:
        purge_derived_configs()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
